    "q-131154": {
      "dom_id": "step-q131154",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[2e-(e-1)X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{e}{e{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}},\\,1<x<e\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[2e-(e-1)X]=2e-(e-1)\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\cdot \\frac{e}{e{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{e}{ex-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{e-1}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{e}{e-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{e-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2e-(e-1)X] & =2e-(e-1)\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =2e-(e-1)\\cdot \\frac{e}{e-1} \\\\  & =2e-e \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
