    "q-152907": {
      "dom_id": "step-q152907",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[12X-8].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{8}{9}, & 1\\le x<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we simplify using the properties of the expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[12X-8]=12\\text{E}[X]-8\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{8}{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8x}{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12})|}_{0}^{1}+{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{12}-0)+(\\frac{16}{9}-\\frac{4}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[12X-8] & =12\\text{E}[X]-8 \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{17}{12}-8 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
